  ) as v(name, sci, cat, short, descr, price, compare, stock, low, img, light, diff, water, feed,
         pet, air, edible, height, tags, featured)
  join public.categories c on c.slug = v.cat
 where not exists (select 1 from public.products p where p.name = v.name);

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Journal
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
insert into public.journal_posts (title, excerpt, body, cover_url, tags, product_ids, published_at)
select v.title, v.excerpt, v.body, v.cover, v.tags,
       array(select id from public.products where name = any (v.products)), now() - v.age
  from (values
    ('Low-light heroes: plants for a dim Dhaka flat',
     'No sunny window? These plants are happy with what you''ve got.',
     E'Most flats in Dhaka face another building a few metres away. That doesn''t mean you can''t have plants — you just need the right ones.\n\n## The shortlist\n\n- **Snake plant** — tolerates almost total neglect and dim corners.\n- **ZZ plant** — stores water in its roots, so missing a fortnight of watering is fine.\n- **Golden pothos** — trails from a shelf and grows even under tube lights.\n- **Peace lily** — one of the few plants that flowers in low light.\n- **Parlor palm and Aglaonema** — soft texture and colour for a north-facing room.\n\n## The one rule\n\nLow light means slow growth, and slow growth means the plant drinks less. **Water half as often as you would for a plant in a sunny window.** Most low-light plants die from overwatering, not from darkness.\n\n## Measure your light\n\nOpen your phone''s camera at midday and point it at the spot. If you can read a book there comfortably without a lamp, you have "medium" light. If you need a lamp, you have "low" light — stick to the plants above.',
     '/plants/snake-plant.webp', array['indoor', 'beginner', 'low-light'],
     array['Snake Plant (Laurentii)', 'ZZ Plant', 'Golden Pothos (Money Plant)', 'Peace Lily', 'Parlor Palm', 'Aglaonema ''Red Siam'''], interval '38 days'),
    ('Why isn''t my Monstera splitting?',
     'Fenestrations, aerial roots and other questions we get every week.',
     E'The split leaves — called *fenestrations* — are the whole point of a Monstera. So why does yours keep putting out plain hearts?\n\n## It''s about maturity and light\n\nYoung Monsteras produce solid leaves. Splits appear once the plant is a couple of years old **and** gets plenty of bright, indirect light. In a dim room, it stays juvenile forever.\n\n## Give it something to climb\n\nIn the wild, Monsteras climb trees. A moss pole tells the plant it''s climbing towards light, and leaf size increases with every new node.\n\n## Those long brown roots\n\nAerial roots are normal. Tuck them into the soil, guide them onto the pole, or trim them — none of these will hurt the plant.\n\n## Yellow leaves?\n\nOne yellow leaf at the bottom is just an old leaf retiring. Several yellow leaves at once usually means the soil has stayed wet for too long.',
     '/plants/monstera.webp', array['indoor', 'care-guide', 'troubleshooting'],
     array['Monstera Deliciosa'], interval '52 days'),
    ('Adenium through the seasons',
     'How to keep a desert rose flowering on a Bangladeshi rooftop.',
     E'Adeniums are the collector''s plant of Dhaka rooftops, and it''s easy to see why: fat sculptural trunks and trumpet flowers in every shade of pink.\n\n## Summer (March–May)\n\nThis is peak season. Full sun, generous watering whenever the soil dries, and a high-phosphorus feed every two weeks.\n\n## Monsoon (June–September)\n\nThe danger season. Constant rain rots the caudex. Move pots under a shelter or tilt them so they drain fast. Water only when the soil is dry.\n\n## Winter (December–February)\n\nGrowth slows and some leaves drop. Water once every two weeks at most. Don''t panic — it''s resting.\n\n## Raising the caudex\n\nEvery time you repot, raise the plant a little so more of the swollen root shows above the soil. Over a few years, this creates the dramatic bonsai-like base everyone admires.',
     '/plants/adenium.webp', array['succulents', 'rooftop', 'seasonal'],
     array['Adenium (Desert Rose)', 'Crown of Thorns'], interval '67 days'),
    ('Growing dragon fruit on your roof',
     'Posts, rings, night flowers and the trick to getting fruit.',
     E'Dragon fruit has become one of the most popular rooftop crops in Bangladesh — and with good reason. It is a cactus, so it shrugs off heat and missed waterings.\n\n## Build the support first\n\nUse a sturdy post about 1.5 m tall with an old tyre or a metal ring on top. The plant climbs the post and its branches drape over the ring. Fruit forms on the hanging branches.\n\n## Soil and pot\n\nA 20-inch drum with a mix of soil, sand and compost. Drainage holes are essential.\n\n## Flowers open at night\n\nThe huge white flowers open after dark and close by morning. Many varieties need hand-pollination: dab the pollen onto the centre of the flower with a soft brush around 9pm.\n\n## When will it fruit?\n\nUsually in its second summer. A healthy plant can then fruit in several waves from May to October.',
     '/plants/dragon-fruit.webp', array['fruit-trees', 'rooftop', 'grow-guide'],
     array['Dragon Fruit (Red Flesh)'], interval '74 days'),
    ('A monsoon survival guide for container plants',
     'Drainage, fungus, and what to do when it simply won''t stop raining.',
     E'The monsoon is wonderful for plants in the ground and hard on plants in pots. Water has nowhere to go.\n\n## 1. Check every drainage hole\n\nLift each pot after a heavy shower. If water pools at the bottom, poke the hole clear or raise the pot on bricks.\n\n## 2. Stop scheduled watering\n\nWater only when the soil is actually dry. During continuous rain that might mean not watering for a week.\n\n## 3. Move the sensitive ones\n\nSucculents, cacti, rosemary and adeniums hate wet roots. Move them under a shade or a balcony overhang.\n\n## 4. Watch for fungus\n\nWhite powder on leaves, black spots or a sour smell from the soil all signal fungal problems. Remove affected leaves and improve airflow.\n\n## 5. Feed lightly\n\nRain washes nutrients out of pots, so a light, diluted feed every two weeks helps leafy plants stay green.',
     '/plants/boston-fern.webp', array['seasonal', 'care-guide', 'monsoon'],
     array['Boston Fern', 'Rosemary', 'Adenium (Desert Rose)'], interval '96 days'),
    ('Your first bonsai: start with a ficus',
     'Bonsai isn''t a mystery — it''s pruning, patience and the right tree.',
     E'Many first bonsai die within a month, usually because they are outdoor trees kept indoors. Choosing the right species makes all the difference.\n\n## Why ficus?\n\nFicus copes with indoor light, bounces back from mistakes and grows fast enough to reward your pruning. A Ficus Ginseng with its thick roots looks impressive from day one.\n\n## Water is the whole game\n\nBonsai pots hold very little soil, so they dry out fast. Check daily by pressing a finger into the soil. Water when the top layer is dry, until water runs out of the bottom.\n\n## Pruning\n\nLet a shoot grow 6–8 leaves, then cut it back to two. This keeps the tree compact and encourages branching.\n\n## When you''re ready for more\n\nOnce you''ve kept a ficus happy for a year, try a Chinese elm or a juniper — both need to live outdoors in full sun.',
     '/plants/ficus-bonsai.webp', array['bonsai', 'beginner'],
     array['Ficus Ginseng Bonsai', 'Chinese Elm Bonsai', 'Juniper Bonsai'], interval '110 days'),
    ('Repotting without the drama',
     'When to repot, which size to choose and how to avoid transplant shock.',
     E'Repotting is less stressful for a plant than living in a pot it has outgrown — as long as you do it right.\n\n## Signs it''s time\n\n- Roots circling the bottom or poking out of the drainage holes\n- Water runs straight through without soaking in\n- The plant dries out within a day or two\n\n## Go up one size\n\nChoose a pot just 2–5 cm wider than the old one. A huge pot holds too much wet soil and rots the roots.\n\n## Step by step\n\n1. Water the plant the day before.\n2. Tip it out and gently loosen the outer roots.\n3. Add fresh mix to the new pot so the plant sits at the same depth.\n4. Fill in around the sides and firm lightly.\n5. Water well and keep it out of direct sun for a week.\n\n## Best time\n\nSpring, just before the main growing season. Avoid repotting during peak summer heat or while a plant is flowering.',
     '/plants/rubber-plant.webp', array['care-guide', 'beginner'],
     array['Rubber Plant (Burgundy)', 'Fiddle Leaf Fig', 'Monstera Deliciosa'], interval '125 days'),
    ('Pet-safe plants for homes with cats and dogs',
     'Greenery without the worry — and a few common plants to avoid.',
     E'Many popular houseplants are mildly toxic if chewed. If you share your home with a curious cat or dog, start with these.\n\n## Safe and easy\n\n- **Spider plant** — cats love to play with the leaves, and it''s harmless.\n- **Parlor palm and Areca palm** — soft fronds and completely pet-safe.\n- **Prayer plant** — patterned leaves that move at night.\n- **Boston fern** — perfect for a shaded veranda.\n- **Haworthia and Echeveria** — succulents that are safe for pets.\n- **Money tree** — a pet-safe gift for a new home.\n\n## Better kept out of reach\n\nPothos, philodendron, monstera, peace lily, snake plant, aloe vera and most lilies can cause mouth irritation or stomach upset. Hang them high or keep them in rooms pets don''t visit.\n\n**Tip:** every plant in our shop shows a "Pet friendly" badge when it''s safe. Filter by it on the Shop page.',
     '/plants/spider-plant.webp', array['indoor', 'pets', 'beginner'],
     array['Spider Plant', 'Parlor Palm', 'Areca Palm', 'Prayer Plant', 'Boston Fern', 'Money Tree'], interval '140 days'),
    ('Bougainvillea won''t flower? Be a little mean to it',
     'Why too much love gives you leaves instead of colour.',
     E'Bougainvillea is one of the most frustrating plants for new gardeners: lush green growth, but hardly any colour.\n\n## The secret is stress\n\nBougainvillea flowers when it is slightly stressed — a bit dry, a bit root-bound, in blazing sun. Too much water and nitrogen fertiliser produce leaves instead.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Move it to the sunniest spot you have: 6+ hours of direct sun.\n- Let the soil dry out between waterings.\n- Switch to a high-phosphorus "bloom" fertiliser.\n- Keep it slightly root-bound — don''t repot into a much bigger pot.\n\n## Prune after each flush\n\nBracts form on new growth, so trim the stems after each flowering wave. You''ll get a fresh flush within six weeks.',
     '/plants/bougainvillea.webp', array['flowers', 'rooftop', 'troubleshooting'],
     array['Bougainvillea (Magenta)'], interval '158 days'),
    ('Winter vegetables to plant in October',
     'Cauliflower, coriander, tomatoes and leafy greens for the cool season.',
     E'From October to February, Bangladesh has the best growing weather of the year. Days are sunny, nights are cool and pests are fewer.\n\n## What to plant\n\n- **Cauliflower** — plant seedlings by early November for January harvests.\n- **Coriander** — sow every three weeks for a steady supply.\n- **Cherry tomatoes** — they love the cool, bright weather.\n- **Lal shak and lettuce** — ready to pick within a month.\n- **Spring onions** — snip and they regrow.\n\n## Prepare the soil\n\nMix compost into last season''s pots and top up with fresh soil. Winter vegetables are hungry.\n\n## Water in the morning\n\nCool nights and wet leaves invite fungal disease. Water at the base, early in the day.',
     '/plants/cauliflower.webp', array['kitchen-garden', 'seasonal', 'winter'],
     array['Fulkopi (Cauliflower)', 'Dhonepata (Coriander)', 'Cherry Tomato', 'Lal Shak (Red Amaranth)', 'Spring Onion'], interval '175 days'),
    ('Make compost from your kitchen scraps',
     'Turn vegetable peels into free fertiliser in eight weeks.',
     E'Around half of household waste in Bangladesh is food scraps. On a balcony, that can become the best fertiliser your plants will ever get.\n\n## What you need\n\nA bucket or drum with a lid and holes drilled in the sides and bottom, plus a tray underneath.\n\n## The recipe\n\nAlternate layers of **greens** (vegetable peels, tea leaves, coffee grounds) and **browns** (dry leaves, shredded newspaper, cardboard). Aim for roughly equal amounts.\n\n## Avoid\n\nMeat, fish, dairy and oily food — they smell and attract pests.\n\n## Keep it working\n\nMix it every few days and keep it as damp as a wrung-out sponge. In Dhaka''s heat, compost is ready in 6–8 weeks: dark, crumbly and smelling of earth.',
     '/plants/banana.webp', array['sustainability', 'kitchen-garden', 'soil'],
     array[]::text[], interval '196 days'),
    ('Five fragrant flowers for a Bangladeshi balcony',
     'Beli, kamini, rajanigandha, gandharaj and madhabilata — evenings, perfumed.',
     E'Some of the most loved plants in Bangladesh are grown more for their scent than their looks.\n\n## Beli\n\nArabian jasmine opens at dusk. A single pot perfumes the balcony all evening.\n\n## Kamini\n\nOrange jasmine blooms in waves after rain, with a sweet citrus scent.\n\n## Rajanigandha\n\nTuberose spikes are a wedding favourite, and the fragrance is strongest at night.\n\n## Gandharaj\n\nGardenia has creamy flowers and one of the richest scents of all. It likes slightly acidic soil.\n\n## Madhabilata\n\nA climber whose flowers change from white to pink to red, with a light honey fragrance.\n\n**Tip:** group them near a window or door so the scent drifts indoors.',
     '/plants/jasmine.webp', array['flowers', 'fragrance', 'balcony'],
     array['Beli (Arabian Jasmine)', 'Kamini (Orange Jasmine)', 'Rajanigandha (Tuberose)', 'Gandharaj (Gardenia)', 'Madhabilata (Rangoon Creeper)'], interval '214 days'),
    ('Succulents in a humid climate: what actually works',
     'Most succulent advice is written for deserts. Here''s the Bangladesh version.',
     E'Succulents store water in their leaves, so humidity plus wet soil is a recipe for rot. But you can grow them well here.\n\n## Choose the right succulents\n\nHaworthia, aloe, jade, crown of thorns and adenium all cope well with humidity. Echeverias need the most care during the monsoon.\n\n## Fix the soil\n\nMix one part potting soil with two parts coarse sand, brick chips or perlite. Water should run straight through.\n\n## Use terracotta\n\nClay pots breathe and dry out faster than plastic.\n\n## Water by weight, not by calendar\n\nLift the pot. If it feels heavy, don''t water. In the monsoon, that might mean once a month.\n\n## Give them sun\n\nStretched, pale succulents are begging for more light. Morning sun is ideal.',
     '/plants/echeveria.webp', array['succulents', 'care-guide', 'monsoon'],
     array['Echeveria ''Mexican Snowball''', 'Zebra Haworthia', 'Aloe Vera', 'Jade Plant'], interval '233 days'),
    ('Fertiliser 101: NPK without the jargon',
     'What the three numbers on the bag mean, and which plants need what.',
     E'Every fertiliser bag shows three numbers, like 10-10-10. They stand for nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P) and potassium (K).\n\n## N — leaves\n\nNitrogen drives green, leafy growth. Leafy greens, herbs and foliage houseplants want more of it.\n\n## P — roots and flowers\n\nPhosphorus supports flowering and fruiting. Use a higher-P feed for adeniums, bougainvillea, hibiscus and tomatoes.\n\n## K — overall health\n\nPotassium strengthens plants against disease and improves fruit quality.\n\n## Organic options\n\n- **Compost and cow dung** — balanced and gentle.\n- **Bone meal** — high in phosphorus, great for flowering plants.\n- **Banana peel tea** — a mild potassium boost.\n- **Mustard cake (khoil)** — soak for a week and dilute it well before using.\n\n## The golden rule\n\nFeed little and often during the growing season, and not at all when a plant is resting or freshly repotted.',
     '/plants/hibiscus.webp', array['soil', 'care-guide', 'fertiliser'],
     array['Joba (Hibiscus)', 'Adenium (Desert Rose)'], interval '262 days')
  ) as v(title, excerpt, body, cover, tags, products, age)
 where not exists (select 1 from public.journal_posts j where j.title = v.title);
